<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cato the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">95–<a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Marcus Porcius Cato Uticensis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cato the Younger (Marcus Porcius Cato) was a Roman senator and Stoic, great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato the Elder</a>, famed for his rigid integrity and defence of the traditional Republic. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> in the civil war. After Caesar's victory at Thapsus in 46 BCE he killed himself at Utica rather than accept Caesar's pardon, earning the name Uticensis and lasting fame as a republican martyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger.jpg" alt="Cato the Younger" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cat%C3%B3n_(52340687524).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
